    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000124704,
      "SummaryText": "Executive Summary\n\nAnhydrous Ammonia Risk Management Plan\nEPA RMP \u0096 Program Level 2\n\nThis Risk Management Plan (RMP) describes the systems and procedures implemented to safely manage anhydrous ammonia, a regulated toxic substance under the EPA Risk Management Program (40 CFR Part 68). The facility is classified as Program Level 2 because an accidental release of anhydrous ammonia could potentially impact offsite receptors, and the process is not subject to OSHA Process Safety Management (PSM) requirements.\n\nRegulated Substance and Process Description\n\nAnhydrous ammonia is stored and handled in pressure-rated vessels and associated piping systems designed, constructed, and maintained in accordance with applicable codes, standards, and recognized and generally accepted good engineering practices (RAGAGEP). The ammonia system supports facility operations and is managed to minimize the risk of accidental release.\nHazard Assessment\n\nA hazard assessment has been conducted in accordance with EPA RMP requirements. This includes:\nA worst-case release scenario, assuming catastrophic failure of the largest ammonia vessel and release of the maximum quantity under specified meteorological conditions.\nAn alternative release scenario, representing a more credible event such as a piping or valve failure.\nThe assessment evaluated potential offsite impacts to public and environmental receptors and is used to guide prevention and emergency planning activities.\nAccident Prevention Program\n\nConsistent with Program Level 2 requirements, the facility has implemented a prevention program that includes:\n\nSafety information covering the hazards of anhydrous ammonia and safe operating limits\nWritten operating procedures addressing normal operations, startup, shutdown, and emergency conditions\nTraining programs to ensure employees are qualified to safely handle anhydrous ammonia\nMaintenance and inspection procedures to ensure the mechanical integrity of tanks, piping, valves, and safety system"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000124704,
      "SummaryText": "s\nA documented compliance audit program conducted at least every five years\nAn incident investigation process to identify root causes and prevent recurrence\nEmergency Response Program\n\nThe facility maintains a written Emergency Response Plan consistent with 40 CFR Part 68 Subpart E. The plan includes procedures for:\nRecognizing and responding to ammonia releases\nEvacuation and shelter-in-place actions\nCoordination with local emergency responders and emergency planning committees\nEmployee training and periodic emergency drills\n\nFive-Year Accident History\n\nThe facility has evaluated its accident history for the past five years as required by EPA RMP regulations. Any qualifying accidental releases, if applicable, have been documented and corrective actions implemented. If no qualifying releases occurred, this is noted in the RMP submission.\nManagement Commitment and Continuous Improvement\n\nFacility management is committed to protecting employee safety, public health, and the environment through effective implementation of this Risk Management Plan. Ongoing reviews, employee involvement, and continuous improvement practices are used to maintain compliance and reduce the likelihood and consequences of anhydrous ammonia releases."
    }
